# kk: stitch consecutive load/store render passes with suspend/resume
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/46515 state=opened
# ### What does this MR do and why?

<!--Describe in detail what your merge request does and why.-->

```
Engines that split a layer across worker threads submit many render passes that load and store the same attachments. On a tile GPU each one round-trips the targets through tile memory. This records such passes as suspending/resuming Metal 4 encoders and commits consecutive ones with matching attachments as a single pass.

Notes for review:

• Multisampled passes are only stitched when they start a command buffer.
• Vertex work in a resuming encoder is not ordered by Metal 4 queue barriers. A fence taken by a small compute encoder before each stitched chain orders it. The second commit narrows that wait to the stages the chain actually depends on.
• Each stitched command buffer gets its own command allocator; reusing one after a stitched commit stalls the queue.
• Disable with MESA_KK_DEBUG=no_merge_passes.

Results (GPU ms, without -> with):

• M1 Pro, a game scene: no MSAA 26.6 -> 24.8, 2x 28.6 -> 26.0, 4x 30.6 -> 28.1. The second commit adds about 1.5%.
• M5 Pro, s&box benchmark: citizens 50.2 -> 37.6, cubes 13.9 -> 8.3, directional light 11.9 -> 8.2. Bloom, spot, static light and AO are 5-7% faster. Most other scenes are within noise.

Known cost: a stitched pass needs an opener, a closer and a fence, so it only pays off when it chains with others. In s&box, light.point is about 6% slower (7.47 -> 7.90 ms) because most of its passes don't chain. With no_merge_passes it is back to 7.51 ms.

Tested: Vulkan CTS 1.4.7.1 renderpasses (81687 cases) and synchronization (146489 cases), no failures and identical results to main. Metal API validation clean on renderpasses (the 130 m10_feedback_loop cases fail the same way on main, pre-existing). I also checked the dEQP-VK renderpasses non-random subset on M5 Pro: identical per-case results on main and this branch.

